<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georgius Agricola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1494–1555</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Georg Agricola, Georg Bauer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georgius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> was a German physician and scholar, often called the father of mineralogy. Working as a town doctor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jachymov">Joachimsthal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a>, he studied the mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a> and wrote De re metallica, published in 1556, a year after his death: a systematic account of mining and metallurgy, illustrated with woodcuts, that remained the standard work for two centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georgius-agricola.jpg" alt="Georgius Agricola" width="310" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: {{creator:|Year = }} · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgius_Agricola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
